"\nTARGET 2 CLAUDE.md prose\u002dentrypoint\nIDENTITY 7a861d5eb9f839560f56de9dce4fe09742ca9e68 ed40b19efa50741f093de96a157086d7676e909246327eba8938da52ea4d1541 RANGES [[1, 210]] MASKED [51]\n1: # CLAUDE.md\n2: \n3: This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n4: \n5: pixelelated is an **immutable Linux distribution for handheld gaming devices** (a ROCKNIX/JELOS fork\n6: built on the LibreELEC/CoreELEC cross\u002dcompilation system). There is no app to run \u2014 this\n7: repo is a *build system* that cross\u002dcompiles a complete OS image (kernel, bootloader,\n8: emulators, userland) per device.\n9: \n10: **Resuming work, or new to the project?** Read `.github/sessions/saved\u002dsession\u002dstate\u002dnext.md`\n11: on `next` first: the work in flight, what is running, the next commands, and a walk\u002dthrough\n12: for an agent who has never seen the project (D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d133). A session loads the rules of the\n13: worktree it starts in, so check that yours carries `next`'s before trusting any of them:\n14: `git diff \u002d\u002dquiet next \u002d\u002d .claude CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md || echo STALE` \u002d\u002d then merge `next` into\n15: a feature worktree, or read the rules from `next` (#367).\n16: \n17: **Canonical deep\u002ddive docs (this file summarizes; they are authoritative):**\n18: \u002d `AGENTS.md` \u2014 fork workflow and non\u002dobvious gotchas, for agents that read it instead of this file.\n19: \u002d `packages/README.md` \u2014 the authoritative `package.mk` format reference.\n20: \u002d `.claude/rules/*.md` \u2014 the canonical scoped guides, **loaded automatically**: a rule with a\n21:   `paths:` glob loads when a matching file enters context, one without loads every session.\n22:   All 30 of them, indexed in `instruction\u002dfiles.md` (which also carries the front\u002dmatter\n23:   standard, D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d009), so nothing is reachable only by accident:\n24: \n25:   | Always (no glob, or `paths: \"**\"`) | Scoped |\n26:   | \u002d\u002d\u002d | \u002d\u002d\u002d |\n27:   | `least\u002dsurprise` \u00b7 `player\u002dlanguage` \u00b7 `time\u002dto\u002dplay` \u00b7 `vm\u002dfirst` \u00b7 `bugs\u002dare\u002dagent\u002dfirst` \u2014 the four principles every interface and sync decision is weighed against | `packaging\u002dand\u002dpatches` (`packages/**`, `projects/**`) |\n28:   | `es\u002dnative\u002dui` \u00b7 `es\u002dplayer\u002dtext` \u00b7 `es\u002dui\u002dstyle\u002dguide` \u00b7 `es\u002dcode\u002dtraps` \u2014 the EmulationStation four: the mechanics \u00b7 the words a player reads \u00b7 how a screen looks \u00b7 the codebase's sharp edges (D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d007/008) | `rclone\u002dcloud\u002dsync` (the rclone package, `rocknix/sources/scripts`, the cloud tools) |\n29:   | `engineering\u002dpractices` \u00b7 `upgrade\u002dand\u002dinstall` \u00b7 `documentation\u002daccuracy` | `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting` (GENERIC_X64, `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/**`, the VM tools) |\n30:   | `fork\u002dworkflow` \u00b7 `worktrees` \u00b7 `device\u002dbuilds` \u00b7 `release\u002dcandidates` \u00b7 `issue\u002dtracking` \u00b7 `decision\u002dregister` \u00b7 `learning\u002dcapture` \u00b7 `instruction\u002dfiles` \u00b7 `ceremonies` \u00b7 `working\u002dprinciples` | `handheld\u002devidence` (device packages, device kernels, `docs/**`) |\n31:   | `adversarial\u002dcouncil` | `council\u002dsubstrate\u002dintegrity` (council artifacts and skills) |\n32:   | | `change\u002dlog` (`projects/ROCKNIX/packages/**`, the running change log) |\n33: \n34:   Two more documents carry interface law and load *nowhere*: `docs/es\u002dmenu\u002dmap.md`\n35:   (where a row belongs \u2014 and D\u002dUI\u002d039: a row added, moved or renamed updates it in the\n36:   same change) and `docs/conflict\u002dwizard\u002dia.md` (the wizard's IA). Open them when the\n37:   work is theirs; `es\u002dnative\u002dui.md` says which owns what.\n38: \n39: ## Current project identity\n40: \n41: The next RC is **pixelelated**, always lowercase (D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d144).\n42: GitHub organization: `pixelelated`; maintainer: `rasteratops`; developer:\n43: `blitterbot`, unchanged. Rasteratops is a character, not the OS name.\n44: Use `/pixelelated` for new cloud setups (D\u002dCLOUD\u002d174/175).\n45: The required OS adoption path is ROCKNIX \u2192 pixelelated; no\n46: fielded Rasteratops migration gate exists. Preserve ROCKNIX stored interfaces,\n47: upstream credits and historical evidence. See `NAMING.md` and\n48: `docs/pixelelated/rename\u002dplan.md` before identity changes.\n49: \n50: Cloud setup uses normal linking and explicit folder selection (D\u002dCLOUD\u002d175).\n51: [MASKED sensitive\u002dlabelled line; not read unredacted]\n52: stay unchanged. Retire automatic cloud\u002dfolder migration and legacy joining or\n53: following under #508; users populate or rearrange their cloud folders themselves.\n54: OS upgrade preservation and normal sync/restore testing still apply.\n55: \n56: ## Build & development commands\n57: \n58: Builds are driven by `PROJECT` (default `ROCKNIX`), `DEVICE`, and `ARCH`. Per\u002ddevice make\n59: targets exist for: `RK3588`, `RK3576`, `RK3566`, `RK3326`, `RK3399`, `S922X`, `SM6115`,\n60: `SM8250`, `SM8550`, `SM8650`, `SM8750`, `H700`, `AMD64` (see `Makefile`; most build both\n61: `arm` and `aarch64`). `make world` builds the primary device set.\n62: \n63: Docker is the recommended way to build:\n64: \n65: ```bash\n66: make docker\u002dimage\u002dpull                   # pull Rasteratops mirror at Makefile's pinned digest\n67: make docker\u002dRK3588                       # full image build for a device\n68: make docker\u002dshell                        # interactive shell in the build container\n69: PACKAGE=retroarch make docker\u002dpackage    # build one package in the container\n70: ```\n71: \n72: Native equivalents (run from a path **without spaces**, **never as root**):\n73: \n74: ```bash\n75: make RK3588                              # device image build\n76: ./scripts/build <package>                # build ONE package\n77: ./scripts/clean <package>                # clean ONE package so it rebuilds\n78: make kconfig\u002dmenuconfig\u002dRK3588           # edit a device's kernel config\n79: scripts/checkdeps                        # verify host build dependencies\n80: ```\n81: \n82: Fast dev loop \u2014 rebuild one package, then remake the image instead of a full build:\n83: \n84: ```bash\n85: make docker\u002dshell                        # skip when building natively\n86: export PROJECT=ROCKNIX DEVICE=RK3588 ARCH=aarch64\n87: ./scripts/clean <pkg> && ./scripts/build <pkg>\n88: ./scripts/install <pkg> && ./scripts/image mkimage   # needs OS_VERSION/BUILD_DATE exported\n89: ```\n90: \n91: Native image/package entrypoints and Docker build targets automatically use\n92: `tools/watch\u002dbuild` and the shared `tools/watch\u002djob` monitor. Each run prints\n93: its private `.build\u002druns/<id>/` log, PID, result and status location; nested\n94: commands reuse it. Builds require a current single\u002duse capacity/retention admission\n95: from `tools/host\u002dmaintenance/build\u002dadmission` (#493); see its adjacent guide.\n96: Use `tools/watch\u002dbuild \u002d\u002dadmission RECEIPT \u002d\u002d COMMAND` for an ad\u002dhoc build.\n97: Non\u002dcompiling QA/record jobs use `\u002d\u002drecord\u002donly`; that mode cannot admit a nested build.\n98: Status recording does not send automatic chat notifications. See\n99: `device\u002dbuilds.md` for lifecycle and frozen\u002dcheckout handling.\n100: Long jobs also need verified result delivery; until off\u002dsession alerts are\n101: configured, supervise actively and announce completion promptly (#395).\n102: Result files belong to their producer: never pre\u002dfill a live owner's exit or\n103: completion receipts. Keep coordinator observations separate (#597).\n104: \n105: Images land in `target/` (`config/path` sets `TARGET_IMG=$ROOT/target`). Deploy to a\n106: networked device by `scp`\u002ding the image tar to `root@<host>:~/.update` and rebooting\n107: (preserves settings).\n108: \n109: **Testing/lint:** there is **no unit\u002dtest suite**. `tools/pkgcheck <package>` is the only\n110: lint \u2014 run it after every `package.mk` edit. The real test is that the package/image builds.\n111: A first build needs ~200GB disk and hours; cached rebuilds take minutes.\n112: \n113: ## Architecture\n114: \n115: **Layered config resolution** (`config/options`): options are sourced in order \u2014\n116: `distributions/<DISTRO>/options` \u2192 `projects/<PROJECT>/options` \u2192\n117: `projects/<PROJECT>/devices/<DEVICE>/options` \u2192 `config/arch.<ARCH>` \u2014 each layer\n118: overriding the last. Device knobs (CPU flags, kernel target, bootloader, GPU family) live\n119: in the device `options` file.\n120: \n121: **Package override model:** every package is a directory with a `package.mk`. A\n122: `package.mk` under `projects/<PROJECT>/packages/...` or\n123: `projects/<PROJECT>/devices/<DEVICE>/...` overrides the generic one of the same name in\n124: `packages/`. `DEVICE_ROOT` lets one device reuse another's build root.\n125: \n126: **Directory roles:**\n127: \u002d `packages/` \u2014 generic cross\u002dproject package recipes, grouped by function.\n128: \u002d `projects/<PROJECT>/` \u2014 SoC/vendor families (`ROCKNIX`, `Rockchip`, `Qualcomm`, ...): device `options`, package overrides, `patches/`, `filesystem/` overlays, `bootloader/`.\n129: \u002d `distributions/ROCKNIX/` \u2014 distro identity (version, options, splash).\n130: \u002d `scripts/` \u2014 the build engine (`build_distro`, `build`, `clean`, `install`, `image`).\n131: \u002d `tools/` \u2014 dev helpers (`pkgcheck`, `distro\u002dtool`, `adjust_kernel_config`).\n132: \u002d `build.*/`, `sources/`, `release/`, `target/` \u2014 gitignored build outputs.\n133: \n134: **Emulator naming:** libretro cores are `*\u002dlr`; standalone emulators are `*\u002dsa`\n135: (`projects/ROCKNIX/packages/emulators/`).\n136: \n137: ## `package.mk` rules (see `packages/README.md` for the full reference)\n138: \n139: \u002d **Late\u002dbinding (enforced by `pkgcheck`):** toolchain/path vars (`CC`, `CFLAGS`, `PKG_BUILD`, `TARGET_*`, ...) exist only *after* the package loads \u2014 reference them **only inside functions** (`configure_package`, `pre_configure_target`, ...), never at global scope.\n140: \u002d Customize via `pre_*`/`post_*` hook functions rather than replacing core build steps; branch per device with `case ${DEVICE} in ... esac`.\n141: \u002d Preserve upstream JELOS/LibreELEC copyright headers and add a ROCKNIX line \u2014 this is a fork; credits must be retained.\n142: \u002d Pin git sources with the **full** commit hash in `PKG_VERSION`.\n143: \u002d Patches in a package's `patches/` dir auto\u002dapply after unpack, and so do\n144:   `patches/<arch>/` (`x86` for an `x86_64` target, `scripts/unpack:127\u002d133`)\n145:   and `projects/<PROJECT>/devices/<DEVICE>/patches/<pkg>/` (`:159`); a\n146:   per\u002ddevice `patches/<DEVICE>/` inside the package applies only when the\n147:   recipe adds it to `PKG_PATCH_DIRS` (`:137\u002d145`; upstream's ES recipe sets\n148:   `PKG_PATCH_DIRS+=\" ${DEVICE}\"`). Kernel patches live under\n149:   `packages/linux/patches/<DEVICE>/`; hardware quirks under\n150:   `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/hardware/quirks/`. (T37 L\u002d18, at `2174265e9c`.)\n151: \n152: ## Commit conventions\n153: \n154: No Conventional Commits. Scope by package or device, matching history:\n155: `azahar\u002dsa \u002d bump to ...`, `SM8250 \u002d linux \u002d enable ntsync`, `emulationstation: bump package`.\n156: **Upstream PR commits are stricter** (CI\u002denforced): `package: text` title matching\n157: `^[a\u002dzA\u002dZ0\u002d9_*./\u002d]+:[[:space:]].+$`, \u226472 chars, blank line before body, no merge commits.\n158: \n159: ## Fork workflow (this working copy is a fork)\n160: \n161: `origin` = `pixelelated/distribution`, `upstream` = `ROCKNIX/distribution`. Full rules in\n162: `fork\u002dworkflow.md` / `worktrees.md`; essentials:\n163: \n164: \u002d Branch `next` = `upstream/next` + a personal overlay (`.claude/rules/`, `docs/`, `plans/`, `.githooks/`, ...). **Never PR `next` upstream.**\n165: \u002d Feature work: branch `feature/<name>` from `next` in a worktree at `../rocknix.worktrees/<name>`; the primary checkout stays on `next`.\n166: \u002d Upstream PRs use a throwaway branch built **by content**: `git checkout next \u002d\u002d <the feature paths>` onto a detached `upstream/next`, one commit. The old `git rebase \u002d\u002donto upstream/next next pr/<name>` recipe is retired \u2014 it produces an empty branch, silently, once the feature has been merged into `next`. `.githooks/pre\u002dpush` guards `pr/*`; it is the backstop, not the plan.\n167: \u002d Issues go on the fork: always `gh \u002d\u002drepo pixelelated/distribution` (upstream has Issues disabled).\n168: \u002d The milestone body is the current ordered plan: current/next work, dependencies and exit evidence. Keep open titles aligned as `M7.P1: ...`; M comes from the milestone name, P from its body. Update both when priorities change; preserve closed titles (`milestone\u002dphase\u002dnaming.md`, D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d139).\n169: \u002d User\u002dfacing behavior changes need a follow\u002dup docs PR to the separate `ROCKNIX/rocknix.org` repo.\n170: \u002d Durable lessons: consider an instruction file under `.claude/rules/` and append a timestamped entry to `docs/work\u002dlogs/<yyyy_mm>\u002dwork_logs/<yyyy_mm_dd>\u002dwork_log.md`. A learning that is a *procedure* becomes a tool or a flag, not prose (`learning\u002dcapture.md` \u00a7 3).\n171: \u002d Decisions go in `docs/decision\u002dregister.md` the same session they are made, and are cited by ID rather than re\u002dargued; the table is **append\u002donly** (`decision\u002dregister.md`). Out\u002dof\u002dband maintainer requests become fork issues the same session, quoting their words (`issue\u002dtracking.md`, D\u002dQA\u002d012).\n172: \n173: ## Non\u002dobvious gotchas\n174: \n175: \u002d **Check upstream early when troubleshooting.** Search relevant issue reports,\n176:   release notes and fixes, compare them with our pinned source, and record dated\n177:   links and applicability in the investigation issue. Similar symptoms are not a\n178:   confirmed match (`engineering\u002dpractices.md`, D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d162).\n179: \n180: \u002d Script\u002donly changes (e.g. `scripts/mkimage`) do **not** trigger an image rebuild \u2014 delete `build.*/.stamps/image/build_target` first.\n181: \u002d Building from a git worktree in Docker requires mounting the main repo's `.git`: `DOCKER_EXTRA_OPTS='\u002dv <main\u002drepo>/.git:<main\u002drepo>/.git'`.\n182: \u002d A network/download failure during a build often surfaces as a **misleading, unrelated\u002dlooking build error** \u2014 check for failed downloads first.\n183: \u002d Before \"fixing\" apparently wrong code, verify design intent via `git log \u002dS`/`git blame` \u2014 several dangerous\u002dlooking patterns are intentional (`engineering\u002dpractices.md`).\n184: \u002d `emulationstation` source lives in a separate git repo; see `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/ui/emulationstation/package.mk` for the extra build steps.\n185: \u002d **Keep each ES change with its flow and screenshot evidence.** Update the\n186:   canonical menu/IA/flow reference and accompanying reviewed happy\u002dpath and\n187:   branch frames for navigation, behavior or screen\u002dcontent changes, including\n188:   copy\u002donly edits. A nonvisual change records the exercised flow and unchanged\n189:   presentation. Source, references and the exact\u002dsource frame index precede\n190:   completion or pin promotion (`es\u002dui\u002dstyle\u002dguide.md`, D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d155/156).\n191: \u002d **Clarity, then brevity, then sized to the space** for every string a player reads, and surprise them as little as possible \u2014 `player\u002dlanguage.md` (D\u002dUI\u002d045) and `least\u002dsurprise.md` (D\u002dUI\u002d042), beside `time\u002dto\u002dplay.md` (D\u002dCLOUD\u002d098): interface \u2192 first frame and exit \u2192 next first frame are measured on every image, and nothing goes on the launch path unless it must.\n192: \u002d **Vocabulary is not decoration.** Four tiers (settings; saves; ROMs and BIOS; game content), two verbs (*back up*, *restore*), *sync* reserved for the automatic behaviour, \"Wi\u002dFi\" hyphenated, the serial comma, *game save* vs *save state* \u2014 `es\u002dplayer\u002dtext.md` \u00a7 Conventions, D\u002dUI\u002d022. Only \"back up\" vs \"backup\" is checked mechanically (`tools/vocabulary\u002dcheck`, the `vocabulary` suite of `tools/vm\u002dqa`).\n193: \u002d **Every build ships onto devices that already have state.** Before publishing, check both the upgrade path (a device keeping its `/storage`) and a clean install \u2014 see `upgrade\u002dand\u002dinstall.md`. A fix that changes what we *write* does nothing for what is already written.\n194: \u002d **Can this be done on the VM?** Asked and answered in writing, in the issue, before\n195:   every test, proof or measurement; only a reasoned no (a real panel, a board's memory,\n196:   a battery, a GPU path) moves it off the VM, and \"a real provider\" is not a no\n197:   (`vm\u002dfirst.md`, D\u002dQA\u002d007/017).\n198: \u002d **A handheld is a person's device, and its cloud is their data.** Nothing runs on one\n199:   without a per\u002daction yes: not a reboot, not a game launch, not injected input, not a\n200:   screenshot, not a sync or upload, not a deletion in their cloud. A general offer of\n201:   device testing is not a standing yes; each test is asked for by name with what it\n202:   writes, sends and leaves behind. Reads need no question. `engineering\u002dpractices.md`\n203:   \u00a7 \"Nothing runs on a person's device without their yes\", D\u002dQA\u002d015, blindspot 38,\n204:   `docs/device\u002dtesting\u002dpolicy.md`.\n205: \u002d **Physical\u002ddevice flashing** \u2014 follow `docs/device\u002dflashing\u002drunbook.md` (pointed to from `device\u002dbuilds.md`): identify the removable card at run time and exclude every system disk; read the raw image back before touching its filesystem; on H700 a fresh card does not boot until the exact device tree is activated as `/dtb.img`.\n206: \u002d **rclone cloud\u002dsync** and **GENERIC_X64 VM QA** have sharp edges \u2014 read their instruction files before touching those areas (filter file is an allowlist; `\u002d\u002ddelete\u002dexcluded` is catastrophic; VM disk must be 16GB+ or first boot breaks in a way that looks like a graphics bug).\n207: \u002d **A worktree is removed with `tools/fork\u002dworktree remove`**, never `git worktree remove \u002d\u002dforce` \u2014 it cannot tell a few hundred MB of checkout from hours of un\u002drecoverable build output (`worktrees.md`, D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d005).\n208: \u002d **Read `.claude/rules/` from `next`, not from your feature worktree.** Feature branches cut from an older base silently lack instruction files added since \u2014 the four ES rule files (`es\u002dnative\u002dui`, `es\u002dplayer\u002dtext`, `es\u002dui\u002dstyle\u002dguide`, `es\u002dcode\u002dtraps`) are absent from older worktrees, so ES work done there proceeds without the guidance they mandate.\n209: \u002d **Headless VM QA** (no desktop on the build host): `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm run \u002d\u002dheadless \u002d\u002ddaemonize <qcow2>`, then `tools/vm\u002dserial` for a root shell and `tools/vm\u002dvisual\u002dqa` with `tools/vm\u002dwalks/` for the screen. SSH is disabled on a fresh image, so serial is the way in; stop the VM by its pidfile, never by `pkill` pattern. Details and the keys in `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md`.\n"
